# {{ project_name }} — build output

{{ project_description }}

Everything in this directory is generated by `osprey build`, and every build
recreates it from scratch. Nothing durable lives here: deleting this directory
is always safe, and an edit made here is lost on the next build.

The files you own live one level up, beside `profile.yml`:

- `profile.yml` — what to build; edit this, then run `osprey build`
- `.env` — this host's API keys and secrets; a value set there survives every
  rebuild (`.env.example` beside it lists the ones this deployment supplies)
- `facility/` — your facility's authored sources: its records, limits,
  vocabulary, simulation, knowledge and benchmark queries
{% if ships_web -%}
- `web/` — the web content: CANOPY screens, collections and theme, and the
  landing page's notices
{% endif %}
What is rendered here: `config.yml` (the resolved settings), the agent's
`.claude/` tree (rules, hooks, and MCP server wiring), `facility.json`, and
under `data/` the views the build writes from `facility/`{% if ships_web %} and a working copy
of your `web/` content{% endif %}.

## Your source tree

One level up, as a preset ships it:

```
facility/                             # The facility's authored sources
├── identity.yaml                     # The facility's code and display name
├── records/                          # places, devices, channels, groups, relations
├── limits.yaml                       # Write limits, one record per channel
├── vocabulary/
│   └── classes.yaml                  # Device classes, their parents and aliases
├── provenance/
│   ├── documents.yaml                # The facility's documents and the system holding each
│   └── policy.yaml                   # Which document kind wins for each relation
├── simulation/
│   ├── models.yaml                   # Each simulated model, its engine and wiring
│   ├── decks/                        # The deck each model runs
│   ├── measurement/                  # The measurement kinds each model offers
│   ├── seeds.yaml                    # Simulated channels' nominal values and noise
│   └── scenarios/                    # Simulation scenarios, one YAML file each
├── knowledge/                        # Markdown knowledge bundle
├── benchmarks/
│   └── channel_finder/               # Benchmark query sources, one per channel-finder pipeline
└── ariel/
    └── vocabulary.yml                # Logbook shorthand -> the words entries use
{% if ships_web -%}
web/                                  # The web content
├── canopy/
│   ├── library/screens/              # CANOPY screens
│   ├── collections/                  # CANOPY collections: screens and views opened together
│   └── theme/                        # CANOPY colour theme
└── landing/                          # Notices on the web terminal's landing page
{% endif -%}
```

A facility file holds data only. An importer writes its own layer under
`facility/imported/<layer>/`: `osprey facility import mml` writes
`facility/imported/mml/`. Edit the authored files; an import rewrites its own
directory.

`osprey build` reads `facility/` and writes the facility file and its views
here, never back there: `facility.json` at the root of this directory, and
under `data/` the limits view `channel_limits.json`, the facts view
`facility_facts.json`, the simulator view `simulator/`, the graph view
`graph/facility.ttl`, the channel-finder view `channel_finder/<mode>.json` and
the benchmarks view `benchmarks/queries.json`. `osprey facility show` prints
the facility the tree builds and each view's path.

The benchmarks view writes `benchmarks/queries.json` from
`facility/benchmarks/channel_finder/`: `in_context_queries.json` for
`in_context`, `tree_queries.json` for every other mode. The query sources do
not reach this directory.

{% if ships_canopy_library -%}
## CANOPY screens

`web/canopy/library/` is the deployment's CANOPY screen library: the control
system screens the web terminal's CANOPY panel lists and opens. Screens live
under `web/canopy/library/screens/` as Screen-IR documents (`*.screen.json`),
one file per screen, addressed by their path without the suffix.

The library is baked into the image: after editing a screen, run
`osprey build` and then `osprey up`; `osprey up` alone does not refresh it.

{% endif -%}
## Database paradigms

`channel_finder_mode` in the build profile picks one of three ways to organize
the same channel namespace as a file; all three are views of the same
facility records. The mode's fourth value, `graph`, is not one of them: it
answers from the facility knowledge graph rather than a channel database. Its
corpus is `data/graph/facility.ttl`, the graph view the build writes from
`facility/`, seeded into the `services.graphdb` store.

- `in_context` — flat: the whole database fits in the agent's context. Best
  for fewer than about 1,000 channels.
- `hierarchical` — nested: the agent navigates level by level, the facility's
  place words by depth, then class, device and leaf. Best for more.
- `middle_layer` — functional: System / Family / Field / Subfield, the way
  operators reason about devices rather than the way the control system names
  them.

`osprey channel-finder validate` and `osprey channel-finder preview` check and
show the active database; pass `--database data/channel_finder/<mode>.json` to
name another. `osprey channel-finder benchmark --model <model>` evaluates
accuracy against `data/benchmarks/queries.json`.

## Run it

```bash
osprey build      # render this directory from profile.yml
osprey up -d      # start the deployment's services
osprey chat       # talk to the agent from the terminal
```

